{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments ["<b>And when you err and do not perform all these commandments</b> – This passage is closed in its meaning, and the masters of the plain sense will err in it, saying that it is a sacrifice for one who did not do what he was commanded to do, and he did so unwittingly. But their words are words of wind, for if so, there would be an obligation to bring a sacrifice for every positive commandment in the Torah when one failed to fulfill all of them and erred in one of them, and there would be an obligation of excision (כרת) for anyone who does not fulfill all of them when he transgresses one of them deliberately, for the verse says, \"all that YHWH has commanded you\" (Numbers 15:23). Moreover, it says here, \"and if it was done unwittingly from the eyes of the congregation\" (Numbers 15:24), for their error is in a deed that they did, not that they sat and did nothing. And likewise, \"that you commit with a high hand\" (Numbers 15:30). Rather, its meaning is: when you err and do not do what HaShem commanded but do the opposite, or you may say: when you err and do not perform His commandments in that which He warned you not to do, for prohibitions in negative commandments are called commandments (מצות), as it says, \"when you sin unwittingly against any of the commandments of YHWH that shall not be done\" (Leviticus 4:2). Now, the obligation of this sacrifice for an unwitting error of the congregation differs from the sacrifice stated in the section of Leviticus, for there one must bring a bull for a sin-offering (ibid. v. 15) and here a bull for a burnt-offering and a he-goat for a sin-offering. Therefore our Rabbis needed to say (Horayot 8a) that this sacrifice is for an unwitting transgression of idolatry. And the language of the verse, without removing it from its plain sense and meaning, would say: when you err concerning all the commandments and transgress everything that HaShem commanded you through Moses, not doing anything of all that He commanded you, you shall bring this sacrifice. And that is why it did not mention here, as it says regarding sin-offerings, \"one of all the commandments of YHWH.\" Now according to its meaning, this is the sacrifice of one who is an apostate (משומד) to the entire Torah unwittingly, such as one who goes and cleaves to one of the nations to act like them and does not wish to be included among Israel at all, and all of this is unwittingly — in the case of an individual, such as an infant taken captive among the nations; and in the case of the congregation, such as if they thought that the era of the Torah had already passed and it was not for eternal generations, or they say as remembered in the Sifrei (Shelach 115): Why did the Omnipresent say this? Is it not that we act and receive reward? We do not act and we do not receive reward! — in the manner that Israel would say and ask Ezekiel, as it says: \"There came men from the elders of Israel and sat before me\" (Ezekiel 20:1) — they said to him: Our master Ezekiel, behold a slave whom his master sold did not leave his domain, etc. Or they may forget the Torah, and this has already happened to us, because of our iniquities, in the days of the wicked kings of Israel such as Jeroboam, when most of the people forgot the Torah and the commandments entirely, as found in the book of Ezra regarding the people of the Second Temple. And this is the usage of the language of the verse: this unwitting error mentioned here concerns the Torah and the commandments in their entirety, and therefore our Rabbis designated for them one commandment through whose unwitting transgression one departs from the category of Israel and from all that is commanded in them, and it is idolatry. And the delimitation of the verse would be: when you err to go after other gods and do not perform anything of all the commandments of YHWH — for one who acknowledges a deity other than His, everything that the glorious HaShem commanded is already nullified with respect to him, whether positive commandments or negative commandments, for if there is a deity other than He, then His fear, His commandments, and all obligation in them are nothing at all. And this passage comes to complete in the priestly Torah the law of an unwitting transgression of idolatry, for this book completes the laws of sacrifices as I explained (at the beginning of the book), and it enters here because they rebelled against the word of HaShem and said, \"Let us appoint a head and return to Egypt\" (Numbers 14:4), to be there in Egypt as they were at first without Torah and without commandments. And behold, this passage came to inform them that even concerning idolatry, atonement is possible for those who err unwittingly, but those who act with a high hand — He will cut them off. And I have already explained this excision (כרת) in the section of Acharei Mot (Leviticus 18:29)."], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {0 "The parenthetical reading (אותו) in the verse quotation appears in some witnesses; I rendered the verse without it as the sense requires."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:06:04.004958399Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}